<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ukiyo-e">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ukiyo-e</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ukiyo-e, meaning 'pictures of the floating world', is a genre of Japanese woodblock prints and paintings that flourished from the seventeenth to the nineteenth centuries. It depicted actors, courtesans, landscapes and scenes of urban life. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> count it among the cultural achievements of the period, noting its later influence abroad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e.jpg" alt="Ukiyo-e" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sharaku · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toshusai_Sharaku-_Otani_Oniji,_1794.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1603 Edo became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, and the chōnin class of merchants, craftsmen and workers, at the bottom of the social order, gained most from the city's rapid economic growth. They patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabuki">kabuki</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, geisha and the courtesans of the pleasure districts, and the term ukiyo came to describe this hedonistic way of life. Painted hanging scrolls known as Portraits of Kanbun Beauties gained popularity around 1661 and marked the beginnings of ukiyo-e as an independent school.</p><p>The earliest ukiyo-e works emerged in the 1670s, with the paintings and monochrome prints of beautiful women by Hishikawa Moronobu. Scholars disagree whether the paintings of Iwasa Matabei belong to the genre, and Japanese researchers in particular often name him as its founder rather than Moronobu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Colour was at first added by hand for special commissions. In 1744 the benizuri-e became the first successes in colour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, using one woodblock for each colour. Okumura Masanobu, who opened a shop in 1707, introduced geometrical perspective to the uki-e genre in the 1740s, and in the 1760s the success of Suzuki Harunobu's brocade prints made full-colour production standard, with ten or more blocks for each print.</p><p>Production was divided among four groups: the publisher, who financed and distributed the prints, the artist, who made the design, the woodcarver and the printer. The artist's ink drawing was pasted to a block of cherry wood and cut away, so the original drawing was destroyed. Prints were impressed on hand-made paper by hand, which allowed gradations of colour that machines could not achieve. Typical subjects were beauties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabuki">kabuki</a> actors, sumo wrestlers and, late in the tradition, landscapes.</p><p>A law of 1790 required prints to carry a censor's seal. Censorship grew stricter, and Utamaro was imprisoned in 1804 for prints of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>. The Tenpō Reforms of 1841 to 1843 suppressed images of courtesans and actors, so artists turned to travel scenes and to birds and flowers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Hokusai's The Great Wave off Kanagawa and Hiroshige's The Fifty-three Stations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a> belong to the 19th century. After the deaths of both masters and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868, ukiyo-e declined sharply: woodblock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> turned to journalism and faced competition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a>, and by the 1890s the tradition was moribund.</p><p>In the West, Carl Peter Thunberg was one of the earliest collectors of Japanese prints, and from the 1870s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japonisme">Japonisme</a> influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-degas">Edgar Degas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edouard-manet">Édouard Manet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Vincent van Gogh</a>. In 1915 the publisher Shōzaburō Watanabe introduced the term shin-hanga, new prints, for a revival that followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ukiyo-e” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The arts flourished under the Tokugawa peace: kabuki and bunraku puppet theatre, the <b>ukiyo-e</b> woodblock prints of artists such as Hokusai and Hiroshige, the hokku verse, ancestor of the haiku, that Matsuo Basho raised to a high art, and an urban popular culture centred on the chonin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Photography, which seemed to devalue the painter’s skill in reproducing reality, and Japanese <b>ukiyo-e</b> prints, with their unusual angles, were further influences.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…and again: the Genji Monogatari Emaki of the 12th century is the earliest extant Japanese picture scroll; Tosa Mitsuoki, who lived from 1617 to 1691, painted complete scrolls closely based on the Heian style; and the tale was a popular theme of <b>ukiyo-e</b> prints in the Edo period.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
